# New Lower Bounds for Even and Odd Values of the Partition Function

**Assembled by AxiomProver.**

Lean formalization of Liuquan Wang's *New Lower Bounds for Even and Odd Values of the Partition
Function* [Wang], including the cited literature results it uses.

## Main results

* **Theorem 1.1**, `Formalization.min_oddPartitionCount_evenPartitionCount_ge`: there is `c > 0`
  with `min {N_odd(X), N_even(X)} ≥ c X^{3/4} / (log X log log X)` for all large `X`.
* **Corollary**, `Formalization.oddPartitionCount_evenPartitionCount_ge_rpow`: for every `ε > 0`,
  `N_odd(X)` and `N_even(X)` are `≫ X^{3/4 - ε}`.
* **Remark 4**, `Formalization.min_oddPartitionCount_evenPartitionCount_ge_weak`:
  `min {N_odd(X), N_even(X)} ≫ X^{3/4} / (log X)²`.
* **Remark 2**: `H(z)³ = j(3z)` with `j = E₄³/Δ = q⁻¹ + 744 + 196884 q + ⋯`
  (`Formalization.heckeH_pow_three_eq_kleinJ`); every holomorphic cube root of `j(3z)` is `ζH`
  with `ζ³ = 1` (`Formalization.eq_rootOfUnity_mul_heckeH_of_pow_three_eq`), and `H` is the one
  normalized by `q⁻¹ + O(q)` (`Formalization.heckeHRootSeries_normalized_iff`,
  `Formalization.heckeH_remainder_isBigO`), the McKay–Thompson series of class 3C of
  Conway–Norton. The Faber polynomials of [BKM, p. 24] are `X`, `X²`, `X³ − 744`, `X⁴ − 992 X`,
  `X⁵ − 1240 X²`, `X⁶ − 1488 X³ + 159768` (`Formalization.faberPoly_hSeries_one` to `_six`).
* **Remark 3**: every argument in (3.3) and (3.4) is a positive integer
  (`Formalization.partitionArguments_positive_integers`), and the examples give an even value
  among `p(282), p(287), p(3)` and an odd value among `p(148), p(128)`
  (`Formalization.partitionCount_282_287_3_one_even`, `Formalization.partitionCount_148_128_one_odd`).
* Lemmas 2.1–2.3, 3.3 and 3.5, Remark 1, and Propositions 3.1, 3.4 and 3.6.

## Cited results

| Manuscript input | Lean |
|---|---|
| Weyl's criterion [Weyl] | `Formalization.weylCriterion` |
| PNT in arithmetic progressions [Davenport] | `Formalization.primeNumberTheoremAP` |
| Lemma 3.2 = [BKMST, Theorem 2.1], for `ξ(x) = α x^{1/2} + β x`, `β ∈ ℚ` | `Formalization.sqrtPrimeEquidistributed` |
| Lemma 3.7 = [BGS, Theorem 2] | `Formalization.primesTranslatedSquaresBound` |
| [CMM, Proposition 6.1], `α₅ = C⁵` | `Formalization.etaCubeEightPowFiveCoeffParity` |
| [CMM, Corollary 6.3], `n = 11`, density `1/16` | `Formalization.etaCubeEightPowElevenDensityPos` |

Their proofs use a Wiener–Ikehara theorem, van der Corput and Vaughan estimates
(`Formalization/ExpSum`), a Selberg `Λ²` sieve, Siegel's theorem, a zero-density estimate, short
Euler products [GS, Lemma 2.1], Jacobi's identity modulo `2`, and Hecke `L`-functions of
`ℚ(√-6)` with Epstein zeta functions. The modularity of `H` on `Γ₀(9)` is proved from the
transformation laws of `η` and `E₄`.

